## Project Overview

Many engineering inverse problems are inherently ill-posed, meaning that multiple parameter combinations may explain the same experimental observations. Traditional optimization methods often provide only a single best-fit solution without quantifying the associated uncertainty. This project aims to develop Bayesian deep learning frameworks capable of efficiently approximating posterior probability distributions while providing reliable uncertainty estimates.

The research combines probabilistic machine learning, information geometry, and deep generative models to accelerate Bayesian inference for computationally expensive engineering systems. Particular emphasis is placed on understanding how the geometry of the posterior distribution influences learning dynamics, uncertainty estimation, and model robustness.

---

## Research Objectives

- Develop scalable Bayesian inference algorithms for engineering inverse problems.
- Investigate variational autoencoders for posterior approximation.
- Compare deep generative models with Markov Chain Monte Carlo methods.
- Study the role of Fisher Information Matrix geometry in probabilistic learning.
- Improve uncertainty estimation for ill-conditioned inverse problems.
- Develop trustworthy Scientific Machine Learning methodologies.

---

## Methodology

The research integrates several probabilistic learning approaches, including:

- Variational Autoencoders (VAE)
- Full-covariance posterior modeling
- Normalizing Flows
- Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC)
- Information Geometry
- Fisher Information Matrix analysis
- Bayesian optimization
- Physics-informed probabilistic learning

The developed frameworks are evaluated on benchmark inverse problems and engineering applications with varying levels of ill-conditioning.

